\f0\fs40 \cf2 Laser peripheral iridotomy is a laser procedure used to treat or help prevent angle closure. In some eyes, the drainage angle inside the eye is very narrow. If that angle becomes blocked, eye pressure can rise suddenly and damage the optic nerve.\
\
This treatment uses a laser to make a tiny opening in the outer part of the iris, the coloured part of the eye. That opening gives fluid inside the eye another pathway to move forward, which can help open the angle and reduce the risk of sudden pressure blockage.\
\
The procedure is usually done in the clinic. Numbing drops are used, and sometimes a pressure-lowering drop is given before or after treatment. Many people notice a brief stinging sensation, small pinches of light, or mild discomfort during the laser.\
\
Afterward, vision may be a little blurry for a short time. Some people notice mild redness, light sensitivity, or a headache or brow ache for a day or two. Anti-inflammatory drops may be prescribed for a short period. A pressure check is often done soon after the procedure.\
\
Laser peripheral iridotomy is generally effective, but it does not always remove all future glaucoma risk. Some people still need ongoing monitoring, additional treatment, or other procedures depending on their eye anatomy.\
\
Risks can include temporary pressure rise, inflammation, a small amount of bleeding, glare or light symptoms, and, rarely, damage to nearby eye structures. Follow-up remains important even after a successful procedure.}